\begin{abstract}

Gas chromatography--mass spectrometry (GC-MS) is the most widely relied-upon
technique for identifying volatile compounds, and decades of EI-MS measurements
have accumulated in the published literature. However, spectra are almost universally shared
as figures embedded in PDF documents, making them inaccessible to programmatic
reuse; existing digitization tools either require manual intervention or are
designed for continuous line plots rather than the discrete bar (stick) spectra
that mass spectrometry produces. We present a two-tier pipeline that recovers
$m/z$--intensity peak lists from such figures by reading the PDF drawing (vector)
layer directly, preserving the exact bar geometry without rasterization, and
falling back to a raster-based image path only when the vector layer is absent.
Extracted peaks are refined by a label-anchored calibration step and screened by an
automated physics-based validation layer, and a parallel batch driver scales the
method to corpus-sized collections. We assess extraction fidelity against a large
reference set of NIST library spectra, cross-pipeline consistency on independently
produced real-instrument spectra, and parallel performance. Reading the vector
layer recovers the published peak patterns with near-lossless fidelity and high
throughput, offering a practical route to unlocking the machine-readable spectral
data currently trapped in the figures of the literature.

\end{abstract}


% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}

GC-MS (Gas Chromatography--Mass Spectrometry) is an analytical technique that
unites the separation of compounds by gas chromatography with their identification
by mass spectrometry. Its applications range from forensic science, drug detection,
and environmental analysis to the identification of unknown compounds. Its reliability rests on electron ionization (EI), which breaks each molecule apart
in a consistent, instrument-independent way: the resulting pattern of fragment
masses behaves like a fingerprint that can be looked up in curated reference
libraries. It is this reproducibility that has made GC-MS the benchmark technique
for identifying volatile compounds \cite{fiehn2016metabolomics}.

The volume of chemical data being produced is growing rapidly, yet much of it
remains locked in formats that are difficult to process programmatically. PDFs in
particular are often described as ``dead-end formats'' because information cannot
be extracted from them efficiently or accurately \cite{stodden2016reproducibility},
and mass spectra are most commonly shared as figures embedded in PDFs. Existing
tools such as WebPlotDigitizer are accurate but tedious to use, particularly across
large collections of files or on dense spectra \cite{rohatgi2020webplotdigitizer}.
For mass-spectra graphs specifically, an accurate and automatic tool for recovering
every data point is still lacking. Moreover, digitizing such figures by
rasterization recovers only a subset of the available information
\cite{stodden2016reproducibility}; we therefore read the PDF drawing (vector) layer
directly rather than its rendered pixels, which preserves the underlying geometry
exactly.

With an effective means of extracting this data, machine-learning approaches to
the identification and classification of unknown compounds could become
substantially more robust, helping to mitigate the incomplete compound coverage of
the NIST database \cite{majewski2026hybrid}.

In this paper, we present a pipeline for extracting $m/z$-versus-intensity data
from bar (stick) mass spectra. Our contributions are:
%
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A vector-first extraction method that recovers exact $m/z$--intensity peaks
        directly from the PDF drawing layer, with no machine learning and no
        rasterization in the common case.
  \item A rasterization-based fallback that handles figures whose vector layer is
        absent or unusable, so the pipeline degrades gracefully rather than failing.
  \item A large-scale round-trip fidelity evaluation comparing ${\sim}50{,}000$
        spectra extracted from NIST-rendered PDFs against the same library entries
        they were rendered from, by cosine similarity; cross-pipeline consistency
        on independently produced, real-instrument spectra is assessed separately.
  \item A parallel batch driver that achieves up to $6.94\times$ speedup and
        917\,PDFs/s throughput with a process pool (Amdahl parallel fraction
        89.7\,\%, theoretical ceiling $9.7\times$).
\end{enumerate}


% ============================================================
\section{Related Work}

The work most relevant to this paper falls into three areas: general-purpose
figure digitization tools, mass-spectral library construction and machine
learning, and vector-layer extraction from PDF documents.

\subsection{Plot and figure digitization}

Early approaches to recovering data from scientific figures focused on
semi-automatic tools that require substantial manual setup.
WebPlotDigitizer \cite{rohatgi2020webplotdigitizer}, the most widely used tool,
requires the user to manually align axes, enter tick values, select the colour of
the target plot, and draw the extraction region for each file. This per-file
overhead is impractical at corpus scale and fails on densely packed or
overlapping curves.

The closest fully automatic prior work is Plot2Spectra
\cite{jiang2022plot2spectra}, which digitizes XANES and Raman spectroscopy
images using an anchor-free object detector for axis alignment and a semantic
segmentation network with optical-flow constraints for line separation.
Plot2Spectra was designed for continuous line plots, where data is encoded as an
unbroken curve. Bar mass spectra are structurally different: each data point is a
discrete vertical stroke at an integer mass, and the per-pixel instance-embedding
approach Plot2Spectra uses for multi-line separation is inapplicable to isolated
bars.
Critically, both Plot2Spectra and WebPlotDigitizer operate on rasterized images,
discarding the exact vector geometry that most PDF-based spectra encode.
We target discrete stick spectra and read the PDF vector layer directly, avoiding
the quantization loss that rasterization introduces.

\subsection{Mass-spectral libraries and machine learning}

The NIST/EPA/NIH Mass Spectral Library is the primary reference for EI-MS compound
identification \cite{nist2023library}. Library search uses the cosine similarity
between an experimental spectrum and each reference entry as the match score, a
metric we adopt for our correctness evaluation \cite{stein1994optimization}.
Despite containing over 350,000 compounds, coverage is incomplete and grows
slowly: each update cycle adds approximately 40,000 new spectra
\cite{majewski2026hybrid}. This gap motivates machine-learning approaches that
predict mass spectra from molecular structure or use library spectra to train
classifiers for compound identification
\cite{majewski2026hybrid}. All such methods require large labelled spectral
datasets, which the published literature contains in the form of figures but not
in a machine-readable form. Our extractor provides a route to recovering this data
at scale, directly addressing the training-data bottleneck.

\subsection{Vector-layer processing in PDF documents}

The PDF specification defines page content as a stream of exact drawing operators:
straight strokes, filled paths, and text positioned in a device-independent
coordinate system \cite{adobe2008pdf}. Reading these operators directly recovers
the renderer's geometric intent without loss. By contrast, rasterizing a PDF
page and re-detecting its geometry with image-processing algorithms introduces
pixel quantization, thresholding noise, and DPI-dependent aliasing.
The data-liberation literature has noted that recovering numerical values from
rasterized figures retrieves only a subset of the encoded information
\cite{stodden2016reproducibility}. By targeting the vector layer as the primary
extraction path, our pipeline avoids this information loss entirely for the 99.9\,\%
of files in our corpus that expose a usable drawing layer, falling back to
rasterization only when no vector layer is present.


