\subsection{Gas Chromatography--Mass Spectrometry}

Gas chromatography--mass spectrometry (GC-MS) is a two-stage analytical instrument
that separates and identifies the chemical components of a sample.
In the first stage, the gas chromatograph vaporises the sample and carries it through a
capillary column using an inert carrier gas.
Because different compounds travel through the column at different rates---governed by
their volatility and affinity for the stationary phase---they arrive at the detector
separated in time, one compound eluting per retention-time window.
In the second stage, each eluting compound enters the mass spectrometer, where an
electron-ionization (EI) source fragments it into charged pieces.
A mass analyser (typically a quadrupole) then sorts those fragments by their
mass-to-charge ratio ($m/z$), and the detector records the abundance of each fragment,
producing a bar-chart spectrum of $m/z$ versus relative intensity with the tallest peak
normalised to 100.
Because EI fragmentation is highly reproducible across instruments and laboratories,
the resulting spectrum is effectively a chemical fingerprint that can be matched against
curated reference libraries for compound identification.

The instrument stores these spectra---one per retention-time peak---as PDF reports.
Each report embeds a vector-graphics rendering of the $m/z$--intensity bar chart, and
it is from these embedded charts that this work automatically recovers the numerical
peak lists.

