% --- Algorithm 1 ---
\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Tier dispatch for mass spectrum extraction}
\label{alg:dispatch}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require PDF file $f$, Boolean flag \textit{cross\_validate}
\Ensure $\{\textit{name},\ \textit{peaks},\ \textit{method},\ \textit{warnings}\}$
\State $\textit{name} \gets \Call{ExtractCompoundName}{f}$
\State $\textit{peaks} \gets \emptyset$;\ \ $\textit{warnings} \gets [\,]$;\ \ $\textit{method} \gets \textsc{vector}$
\Try
  \State $\textit{peaks} \gets \Call{VectorPipeline}{f}$
\Catch{ExtractionFailure}
  \State $\textit{method} \gets \textsc{raster}$
  \State $\textit{peaks} \gets \Call{RasterPipeline}{f}$
\EndTry
\State $\textit{warnings} \gets \Call{PhysicsChecks}{\textit{peaks}}$
\If{$\textit{cross\_validate}$ \textbf{and} $\textit{method} = \textsc{vector}$}
  \State $\textit{peaks}_r \gets \Call{RasterPipeline}{f}$
  \State $\Delta_n \gets \dfrac{\bigl||\textit{peaks}| - |\textit{peaks}_r|\bigr|}{\max(|\textit{peaks}|,\,|\textit{peaks}_r|)}$
  \If{$\Delta_n > 0.20$}
    \State $\textit{warnings}.\Call{Append}{\texttt{count mismatch}}$
  \EndIf
  \State $M_v \gets \{p.mz \mid p \in \textit{peaks}\}$;\ \ $M_r \gets \{p.mz \mid p \in \textit{peaks}_r\}$
  \State $\textit{only\_v} \gets \{m \in M_v \mid \nexists\, m' \in M_r : |m - m'| \leq 1\}$
  \State $\textit{only\_r} \gets \{m \in M_r \mid \nexists\, m' \in M_v : |m - m'| \leq 1\}$
  \If{$|\textit{only\_v}| > \max(1,\ 0.10 \cdot |M_v|)$}
    \State $\textit{warnings}.\Call{Append}{\texttt{m/z only in vector}}$
  \EndIf
  \If{$|\textit{only\_r}| > \max(1,\ 0.10 \cdot |M_r|)$}
    \State $\textit{warnings}.\Call{Append}{\texttt{m/z only in raster}}$
  \EndIf
\EndIf
\State \Return $\{\textit{name},\ \textit{peaks},\ \textit{method},\ \textit{warnings}\}$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Vector extraction}

The following describes how the pipeline reads the PDF drawing layer to recover
the exact bar geometry of a mass spectrum, from library selection through
axis calibration and peak extraction.

\paragraph{Choice of PDF library.}
Both tiers are built on PyMuPDF\footnote{PyMuPDF and the underlying MuPDF
library are dual-licensed under the AGPL and a commercial
license~\cite{pymupdf}.} (the \texttt{fitz} binding to the MuPDF C library).
The two-tier design requires three operations from a single library in a common
coordinate system: extraction of the vector drawing operators
(\texttt{get\_drawings}), positioned text extraction for tick-label calibration
(\texttt{get\_text("words")}), and page rendering for the raster fallback
(\texttt{get\_pixmap}). Among widely used Python PDF libraries, only PyMuPDF
exposes all three: pikepdf, PyPDF2, and pdfrw provide neither vector-graphics
extraction nor page rendering, and pdfplumber/pdfminer provide only limited
vector extraction and no rendering~\cite{pymupdf}. Performance further motivates
the choice at corpus scale. On the maintainers' reference benchmark of $7{,}031$
pages, PyMuPDF extracts text in $8.0$\,s, against $101.6$\,s for PyPDF2 and
$227.3$\,s for PDFMiner---a $13$--$28\times$ advantage---and renders pages in
$367$\,s, against $646$\,s for XPDF and $852$\,s for PDF2JPG~\cite{pymupdf}.
Because per-file parsing lies on the critical path of the parallel driver
(\S5.5), this margin directly affects achievable throughput.

PDFs store graphical content as a sequence of vector drawing operations. We
collect each drawing operation and flatten every straight stroke --- both line
items and the edges of rectangles --- into a set of line segments expressed in the
native PDF coordinate system. Because each stroke encodes a shape's geometry
exactly, this recovers the positions of the axes and bars directly, with no
rasterization, thresholding, or learned detection, and hence without the
quantization error those steps introduce.

\textit{Axis and baseline detection.} Each spectrum is a 2-D $x$--$y$ graph, so
the first elements to identify are the two axes. The $x$-axis (the intensity
baseline) is taken as the longest near-horizontal segment in the lower half of the
page; the $y$-axis is the leftmost sufficiently long near-vertical segment. Their
intersection defines the plot origin. A segment is treated as horizontal when its
endpoints differ by less than $0.5$ PDF units in $y$, and as vertical when they
differ by less than $0.5$ PDF units in $x$. Writing $W$ and $H$ for the page
width and height (with $y$ increasing downward), the $x$-axis is the longest
horizontal segment exceeding $0.3\,W$ in length whose $y$-midpoint lies below the
top $30\,\%$ of the page; the $y$-axis is the \emph{leftmost} vertical segment
exceeding $0.15\,H$ in length whose $x$-midpoint lies within the left $0.4\,W$,
with a $0.02\,W$ margin excluded at each page edge so that a full-page border
rectangle is not mistaken for the axis. The leftmost (rather than longest)
vertical is taken because a base peak at $100\,\%$ can be exactly as tall as the
axis. These layout constraints are empirically chosen.

\textit{Calibration.} Mapping pixel positions to $m/z$ values is performed using
the numeric tick labels printed below the $x$-axis. Each label yields a pair
$(x_i, v_i)$, where $x_i$ is the pixel abscissa of the label's centre and $v_i$
its parsed numeric value; in the example of Fig.~3 these are the ticks at
20, 40, \ldots, 320. A least-squares fit over all such pairs gives the linear
calibration
%
\begin{equation}
  m/z = \frac{x - x_0}{s_x}, \label{eq:calib-mz}
\end{equation}
%
where $s_x$ is the number of pixels per mass unit and $x_0$ is the pixel abscissa
corresponding to $m/z = 0$. The intensity axis is calibrated from the ``100''
label at the top of the $y$-axis, which defines the 100\,\% level, together with
the baseline at 0\,\%:
%
\begin{equation}
  I = \min\!\left(\frac{h}{s_y},\ 100\right), \label{eq:calib-i}
\end{equation}
%
where $s_y$ is the fitted number of pixels per intensity percent and $h$ is a
bar's pixel height above the baseline; the clamp at 100 guards against a stroke
that slightly overshoots the labelled maximum.

\textit{Bar extraction.} With axes and calibration established, candidate bars are
the vertical segments that touch the $x$-axis baseline, lie to the right of the
$y$-axis, and fall within the plot bounds. Each segment's abscissa and height
above the baseline give a provisional $m/z$ and an intensity through Eqs.~(1) and
(2) respectively; the $m/z$ assignment is refined by the next step. Segments whose
abscissae coincide to within a tolerance of 0.01\,PDF units are merged into a single
peak, and when multiple strokes share a mass the minimum height among them is taken,
so that a spurious over-tall stroke (e.g.\ a gridline or annotation coincident with
a bar) cannot inflate the reported intensity.

\textit{Label-anchored $m/z$ correction.} The tick calibration of Eq.~(1) is
coarse: ticks are sparsely spaced and each carries sub-pixel position error, so a
small slope/intercept drift accumulates across the axis. On some real-instrument
exports this manifests as a near-uniform rightward drift of ${\approx}0.45$ mass
units, enough to push affected bars across the $0.5$ rounding boundary and assign
the wrong integer mass (e.g.\ $m/z\,4{\to}5$, $17{\to}18$, $29{\to}30$). Every
genuine peak, however, has its integer $m/z$ printed as a text label directly above
its bar; these labels are the instrument's own ground-truth assignment. We recover
them from the text layer, keeping only integer words in $(1, 2000]$ that sit above
the $x$-axis and horizontally over a bar (rejecting tick labels, the $y$-axis
``100'', and scan annotations such as ``2.09e9''), and match each to its nearest
bar to form anchor pairs $(x_j, v_j)$ of bar abscissa and labelled mass.
Rather than a least-squares fit --- which a single mislabelled anchor would skew ---
we fit a robust Theil--Sen line $x = a\,(m/z) + b$, taking $a$ as the median of all
pairwise anchor slopes and $b = \mathrm{median}(x_j - a\,v_j)$. The estimator is
dependency-free, threshold-free, deterministic, and tolerates up to ${\approx}29\,\%$
arbitrary outliers, so a stray label cannot corrupt the assignment. Every bar ---
labelled or not --- is then re-rounded against this clean line, inheriting the
corrected slope and intercept. When fewer than three usable anchors are found the
fit is left untouched and the original tick calibration is retained with a warning,
so the correction never degrades a well-calibrated page. This step is what removes
the residual $m/z$ drift observed on the in-house exports; on the NIST corpus,
where rendering is clean, anchors and tick calibration already coincide.

\textit{Base-peak renormalisation.} The ``100'' label's text-box centre sits a
small, fixed distance above the true 100\,\% gridline, so Eq.~(2) systematically
under-reads every bar by a constant offset $\delta = 100 - \max_k I_k$, where
$I_k$ are the scaled intensities. We remove it by adding $\delta$ back to every
peak (clamped at 100), which restores the base peak to exactly 100\,\% as a mass
spectrometer reports it. The correction is a single additive shift applied
uniformly to all bars; it is skipped when the maximum already meets 100\,\%.

