\subsection{Raster fallback}

When no usable vector layer is present, the pipeline falls back to image
processing. This section describes that image-based path.

The page is rasterised at 600\,DPI --- double the typical 300\,DPI print resolution
--- to ensure that adjacent bars remain visually distinct and no peak is lost to
sub-pixel mixing. The resulting image is converted to greyscale and binarised
using Otsu's method, which adaptively selects an optimal foreground/background
threshold for each document, providing robustness across differently rendered PDFs.

Axis detection proceeds on the binary image by projecting pixel mass onto rows and
columns. The $x$-axis row is the lowest row with a dominant horizontal ink mass;
the $y$-axis column is the leftmost column in the left half of the image with
sufficient vertical ink mass. Tick labels are extracted from the PDF text layer and
projected into image coordinates to produce the same $(x_i, v_i)$ calibration
pairs as the vector pipeline, yielding the least-squares calibration of Eq.~(1) and
the intensity scaling of Eq.~(2).

Bar detection differs from the vector tier, because the raster image has no explicit
stroke geometry to read. Rather than scanning for contiguous black runs --- which
merge adjacent bars into one when the per-mass pixel pitch is small, as it is on
wide-range or high-$m/z$ spectra --- the scanner samples on the calibrated mass grid:
for each integer $m/z$ it computes the expected column, searches a window narrower
than half the inter-bar pitch, and records the height of the tallest bar in that
window. This gives every integer mass its own reading and prevents touching bars
from being collapsed or mis-centred. The scan row is first stepped just above the
(several-pixel-thick) baseline so the axis line itself is not read as one continuous
bar.

The detected bars then pass through the same post-detection processing as the vector
tier without modification: the label-anchored Theil--Sen $m/z$ correction and the
base-peak renormalisation of \S3.2. Because tick labels come from the PDF text
layer, this tier handles figures whose vector drawings are absent but whose text
layer survives; PDFs produced by scanning a physical page carry neither layer and
are outside the scope of both tiers (see \S6.4).

\subsection{Validation layer}

Extracted peaks are subjected to two layers of automated validation: per-file
physics and consistency checks, and a cross-pipeline comparison when both
pipelines are run.

\textit{Physics and completeness checks.} Five invariants are verified against
every extracted spectrum, each chosen because its violation maps to a specific,
diagnosable extraction failure.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textit{Base-peak normalisation.} A mass spectrometer normalises its
    output so that the tallest bar --- the base peak --- is reported at 100\,\%.
    A maximum intensity below 99.5\,\% indicates a $y$-axis calibration failure.
  \item \textit{$m/z$ range $[1, 2000]$.} Values outside this range indicate that
    $x$-axis tick labels were misread during calibration.
  \item \textit{Intensity range $(0, 100]$.} Zero or negative values indicate a
    spurious sub-baseline segment; values above 100\,\% indicate that the
    intensity clamp failed.
  \item \textit{Minimum peak count.} Fewer than three peaks almost certainly
    indicates that the bar scanner missed most of the chart.
  \item \textit{Duplicate $m/z$ values.} A unit-resolution instrument produces at
    most one bar per integer mass; duplicates indicate calibration error of less
    than 0.5 mass units.
\end{itemize}

\textit{Batch-level outlier detection.} Files whose peak count deviates by more
than $6\sigma$ from the batch mean are flagged for manual inspection, catching
systematic failures that pass the per-file thresholds.

\textit{Cross-pipeline validation.} When cross-validation is enabled, both tiers
are run on each file and their outputs compared via Algorithm~1. Two discrepancies
trigger a warning: a peak-count difference exceeding 20\,\% of the larger count,
and $m/z$ sets differing by more than 10\,\% after applying a $\pm 1$ mass-unit
tolerance.

\subsection{Parallel batch driver}

Processing a large corpus efficiently requires overlapping disk I/O with CPU
computation and minimising serialisation overhead. The driver achieves this through
three coordinated design choices.

\textit{Sequential byte-preloading.} The main thread reads every PDF into memory
sequentially before dispatching to workers, amortising filesystem overhead across
the whole batch. Workers receive raw bytes and perform no disk I/O themselves.

\textit{Bounded-window submission.} When a task is submitted to a worker pool it
returns a \emph{future}---a lightweight handle that the caller can later query to
retrieve the result once the worker finishes.
A sliding window of at most $N$ in-flight futures (default $N = 64$) caps peak
memory at approximately $N \times \bar{f}$, where $\bar{f}$ is the mean PDF size,
while keeping the worker pool continuously saturated.

\textit{Single streamed output.} All JSON serialisation is performed by the main
thread, appending each result as one line to a single open JSONL file. This
eliminates the per-file open/allocate/close cycle that dominates wall time at
scale, and the output is readable before the batch completes.

Process pools are recommended for all workloads. The benchmark results of \S5.5
show that process-level isolation bypasses the GIL and achieves $6.94\times$
speedup ($f_{\text{par}} = 89.7\,\%$). Thread pools provide no wall-clock
reduction on this pipeline: Python result-collection and JSONL serialisation in
the main thread remain GIL-bound, regardless of GIL-releasing C extensions in the
worker code.


% ============================================================
\section{Experimental Setup}

We evaluate the pipeline on two datasets: a large NIST-derived set for extraction
fidelity and a small set of independently produced real-instrument spectra for
cross-pipeline consistency on data produced outside the NIST rendering conditions.

\subsection{Datasets}

Two datasets are used, one for fidelity evaluation and one for
cross-pipeline consistency on independently produced data.

\textit{NIST.} Approximately 50,000 EI-MS reference spectra rendered to PDF,
sourced from the NIST MS library \cite{nist2023library}.

\textit{In-house.} A collection of real-instrument gas spectra (provided by IISc)
with no NIST reference, evaluated by vector--raster agreement; disagreements were
checked manually. The collection contains 68 unique spectra used for all reported statistics.

\subsection{Matching protocol (NIST)}

This subsection describes how each extracted NIST spectrum is paired with
its ground-truth library entry and scored.

Reference peak lists were obtained by exporting the $m/z$--intensity values of all
${\sim}267{,}000$ library compounds from NIST MS Search v2.3. Of 49,351 extracted
spectra carrying a parseable compound name, 616 were discarded as duplicate names,
leaving 48,576 matched to a NIST library entry and scored by cosine similarity.

\subsection{Metrics}

We report metrics along two axes: extraction correctness and processing
performance.

\textit{Cosine similarity.} Following standard mass-spectral library search
\cite{stein1994optimization}, each extracted spectrum is scored against its NIST
reference by
%
\begin{equation}
  \cos(a, b) = \frac{\displaystyle\sum_{m \in a \cap b} a_m\, b_m}
                    {\sqrt{\displaystyle\sum_m a_m^2}\ \sqrt{\displaystyle\sum_m b_m^2}}. \label{eq:cosine}
\end{equation}
%
A score near 1.0 reflects faithful recovery of the published peak pattern. This
metric is intensity-weighted over shared masses: it measures fidelity to the figure
as drawn, not chemical correctness of the underlying spectrum.

\textit{Base-peak agreement.} A binary per-compound check of whether the extracted
base peak (maximum-intensity $m/z$) coincides with the reference base peak.

\textit{Physics pass-rate.} The fraction of extracted spectra satisfying all five
validation invariants of \S3.4.

\textit{Parallel performance.} For $P$ workers we report speedup and efficiency,
%
\begin{equation}
  S = \frac{T_1}{T_P}, \qquad E = \frac{S}{P}, \label{eq:speedup}
\end{equation}
%
and estimate the parallel fraction $f$ via Amdahl's law,
%
\begin{equation}
  S(P) = \frac{1}{(1 - f) + f/P}, \label{eq:amdahl}
\end{equation}
%
from which the asymptotic speedup ceiling $1/(1 - f)$ follows. We estimate $f$ by
inverting Eq.~(5) at the measured endpoint speedup $S(P_{\max})$; the plotted
Amdahl curve is therefore a single-point estimate anchored at $P_{\max}$, not a
least-squares fit over the worker ladder.

\subsection{Hardware and software}

All experiments were conducted on a single machine; its specification is given
here for reproducibility.

All experiments were run on an Intel Core Ultra~9 185H, with 22 logical cores and
32.0~GB of RAM, under Windows~11, using Python~3.12.10. Key library
versions: PyMuPDF (\texttt{fitz})~1.27.2.3~\cite{pymupdf}, OpenCV~4.13.0,
NumPy~2.4.4.


