\section{Results}

This section reports the outcomes of the four evaluations: extraction fidelity
on the NIST corpus, pipeline-tier usage, validation-layer pass rates,
cross-pipeline consistency on real-instrument data, and parallel performance.

\subsection{Fidelity against NIST}

The primary correctness evaluation compares each extracted spectrum against
its NIST library entry using cosine similarity, as described in \S4.3.

Of the 49,351 extracted spectra that carried a parseable compound name, 616 were
discarded as duplicate names, leaving 48,576 matched to a NIST library entry.
Across these, the pipeline reproduces the reference peak pattern with near-perfect
fidelity: 48,574 of 48,576 spectra (99.99\,\%) attain a cosine similarity of at
least 0.99, and the extracted base peak coincides with the reference in the same
48,574 cases.

The distribution is sharply bimodal (Table~1, Fig.~4). No spectrum falls in the
intermediate 0.90--0.99 range; every spectrum either matches near-perfectly or
fails outright. The two failures are large, structurally complex molecules:
\textit{2-Acetamido-5-p-tosylamido-p-benzoquinone} yielded only 2 of its 93
reference peaks (cosine $= 0.065$), and \textit{Benzo[g]pteridine,
2,4-diamino-6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-7-methyl-} yielded 72 of 125 (cosine $= 0.278$).
Both show base-peak disagreement. Manual inspection of these PDFs reveals that
the mismatch originates in the source files themselves: the PDFs sourced from
outside the main NIST rendering pipeline contain an atypical layout that the
extractor processed without error, but the peak geometry as drawn does not
faithfully represent the underlying library entry. The discrepancy is therefore
an artefact of the input PDF, not a failure of the extraction logic. These cases
are examined in \S6.3.

The NIST PDFs are rendered from the same library used as the reference, so a high
cosine measures extraction \emph{fidelity} --- whether the drawn geometry is
recovered without loss --- not generalisation to spectra from other instruments.
Cross-pipeline consistency on real-instrument data is assessed in \S5.4.

