\subsection{Cross-pipeline consistency on real-instrument data}

The in-house set carries no external reference, so it cannot measure extraction
accuracy directly; what it provides is a check of whether the two independent
extraction paths --- vector and raster --- converge on the same peak list for data
produced outside the NIST rendering conditions.

A prerequisite for a meaningful comparison is correct $m/z$ assignment.
Unlike the NIST PDFs, which are rendered under controlled conditions, the in-house
exports carry a near-uniform ${\approx}0.45$-unit rightward drift in the tick axis.
Left uncorrected, this drift pushes several bars across the $0.5$ rounding boundary
and assigns the wrong integer mass (e.g.\ $m/z\,4{\to}5$, $17{\to}18$), which
would cause apparent disagreement between the two tiers even when both correctly
read the same drawn bar. The label-anchored Theil--Sen correction of \S3.2
eliminates this drift by fitting a robust regression line through bar-position--label
anchor pairs and re-rounding every bar against its corrected abscissa. It is this
correction that makes the cross-pipeline comparison meaningful for the in-house
data; without it, the m/z misassignment would artificially suppress cosine
similarity regardless of how accurately each tier detected the bar heights.

Both tiers were run on the 68 unique in-house gas spectra after applying the
correction, and compared by cosine (Eq.~3). The mean agreement is 0.9963 and
63 of 68 spectra (92.6\,\%) achieve $\geq 0.99$ (Table~4); base-peak $m/z$
coincides in all 68/68 (100.0\,\%). The distribution is slightly broader than the
NIST result, reflecting greater variability in rendering across independent
instrument software, but no file falls below 0.90. Because both tiers share the
Theil--Sen correction, their agreement reflects consistency of bar detection rather
than of calibration. The lowest-agreement files are
\textit{H2\_70\%\_CO\_15\%\_CO2\_15\%\_03.pdf} (cosine $= 0.9287$, BP$+$) and the
\textit{Methane\_UHP} spectra (cosine $= 0.9363$, BP$+$); these are
characterised in \S6.3.

