result wherever both apply.

\subsection{Parallel performance}

This subsection evaluates how well the parallel batch driver scales with
worker count, comparing thread and process pools.

The batch driver was benchmarked on 17,068 PDFs (corpus folders F1--F17 plus the
in-house gas set) across $P \in \{1, 2, 4, 8, 11, 16, 22\}$ workers on a
22-logical-core machine (Windows~11, Python~3.12.10), three trials per
configuration, comparing thread and process pools (Table~5, Fig.~5). This subset
keeps the full sweep (two pools $\times$ seven worker counts $\times$ three trials)
tractable; because wall time is dominated by per-file parsing, which is independent
of corpus size, the per-worker scaling it measures carries over to the full corpus.

The thread pool achieves no meaningful speedup: wall time rises slightly from
129.2\,s at $P=1$ to 148.5\,s at $P=22$ (speedup $1.00\times$--$0.87\times$,
$f_{\text{par}} \approx 0\,\%$). Although PyMuPDF, OpenCV, and NumPy release
the GIL during C-level work, Python result-collection and JSONL output retain it,
serialising these phases under all worker counts.

The process pool bypasses the GIL entirely: wall time falls from 129.3\,s at
$P=1$ to 18.6\,s at $P=22$ ($6.94\times$, 917\,PDFs/s), with
$f_{\text{par}} = 89.7\,\%$ (serial fraction 10.3\,\%) and Amdahl ceiling
$9.7\times$. The $P=2$ configuration achieves 102.7\,\% efficiency
(superlinear), a process-startup cache-warming artefact. The marginal-return
sweet spot is $P=2$--$4$ (89.9\,\%--102.7\,\% efficiency); absolute speedup
continues rising through $P=22$.

