% ============================================================
\section{Discussion}

The results presented in \S5 raise several questions of interpretation: why
the vector-first approach achieves near-lossless fidelity, what the residual
failures reveal about the pipeline's limits, and where the method will not
generalise. These are addressed in turn below.

\subsection{Why the vector-first design works}

This subsection explains why reading the PDF vector layer directly produces
higher fidelity than image-based approaches on the same data.

A PDF drawing layer is not an approximation of a spectrum --- it is the exact
sequence of coordinates the rendering software used to place each bar. Reading it
recovers bar positions at floating-point precision: there is no pixel grid to
quantize against, no threshold to tune, and no learned detector to fail on
unfamiliar rendering styles. When calibration tick labels are also present in the
text layer, the $m/z$--pixel mapping is a linear fit to exact positions rather than
to blob centroids. The round-trip error reduces to floating-point rounding in the
least-squares fit, which is negligible at the scale of integer mass units.

The corpus validates this at scale. The vector tier handled 49,970 of 49,999
files (99.9\,\%), and among these 48,574 of 48,576 matched spectra reached cosine
$\geq 0.99$ with base-peak agreement. The two failures are missed-extraction
events --- the calibration was applied correctly, but a small number of bars were
not detected --- rather than calibration errors that would shift every peak
proportionally. The absence of any cosine in the 0.90--0.99 range confirms that
the pipeline either recovers a spectrum faithfully or produces a sparse result
that is identifiably incomplete.

The parallel performance result reinforces the same independence principle.
Because each file is processed without shared state, the work is embarrassingly
parallel; process-level isolation removes the GIL as a serialisation bottleneck
and achieves $6.94\times$ speedup with an 89.7\,\% parallel fraction.
The 10.3\,\% serial residual reflects the minimum coordination cost ---
sequential preloading and single-writer output --- that is inherent to any
corpus-scale pipeline.

\subsection{Ablations and sensitivity}

Formal component-isolation ablations were not conducted because the near-perfect
NIST result leaves little variance to attribute. Each component's contribution
is instead characterised through two indirect sources of evidence.

The validation layer provides per-check coverage: zero spectra triggered a
physics failure across 49,999 files, confirming that calibration produced no
out-of-range outputs. Of the 32 empty-extraction warnings, all 29 raster-fallback
files came up empty --- showing that the raster tier is invoked precisely on files
the vector tier cannot handle --- and the remaining 3 are vector files in which no
bars were detected. The 20 high-outlier alerts (all passing physics checks)
represent genuinely dense spectra, confirming that the $6\sigma$ threshold
correctly identifies statistical outliers.

The in-house cross-validation serves as an indirect sensitivity test of the raster
tier. A mean vector--raster cosine of 0.9963 across 68 unique independently produced
files (68/68 base-peak agreement) shows that the two tiers converge on the same peak
list in almost all cases, with disagreements being diagnosable rather than random.

\subsection{Failure analysis}

The following examines the small number of cases where the pipeline produced
a low-agreement result, separating failures attributable to the source data
from those attributable to extraction behaviour.

\textbf{NIST failures.} Both failure cases originate in the source PDF, not in
the extraction logic. The charts embedded in these files are themselves
incomplete: they do not render the full set of peaks recorded in the NIST library
entry. The extractor correctly reads what is drawn --- calibration is accurate and
recovered peaks sit at the correct $m/z$ values --- but because the chart omits
most of the spectrum, the extracted peak list is necessarily sparse.
\textit{2-Acetamido-5-p-tosylamido-p-benzoquinone} has only 2 bars present in the
PDF against 93 in the reference (cosine $= 0.065$, BP$-$);
\textit{Benzo[g]pteridine, 2,4-diamino-6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-7-methyl-} has 72 bars
against 125 (cosine $= 0.278$, BP$-$). The low cosine similarity is the expected
outcome when the input chart is incomplete, and it is the correct behaviour of the
pipeline: a faithful extractor cannot recover peaks that were never drawn. Both
files are correctly flagged by the validation layer (Fig.~6).

