\textbf{In-house disagreements.} All 68 unique files agree on the base peak and
none falls below cosine 0.90; the residual disagreements are confined to
low-intensity peaks that one tier detects and the other misses. The lowest-agreement
case, \textit{H2\_70\%\_CO\_15\%\_CO2\_15\%\_03.pdf} (cosine $= 0.9287$, vector 20
peaks, raster 16 peaks, BP$+$), and the \textit{Methane\_UHP} spectra (cosine
$= 0.9363$, vector 20 peaks, raster 16, BP$+$) both arise from the vector tier
recovering small fragment peaks the raster grid-sampler misses: for methane,
vector resolves $m/z$ 14 (CH$_2^{+}$) at 67.7\,\% relative intensity, which the
raster tier drops, accounting for almost the entire cosine gap. Because the
vector layer encodes the bar exactly, these cases are evidence of vector
\emph{recovering} information that rasterization discards, rather than of an
extraction error.

\subsection{Limitations}

No extraction pipeline is universal. This subsection identifies the
conditions under which the current approach will degrade or fail, and
suggests directions for future work to address them.

\textit{Requires a vector or text layer.} PDFs produced by scanning carry neither
vector drawing operators nor a selectable text layer; the raster path can detect
bars but calibration fails without legible tick labels. The 29 raster-fallback
empty extractions in the corpus illustrate this boundary. Future work should
incorporate an OCR-based tick-label path.

\textit{Assumes unit-resolution integer $m/z$.} The calibration rounds every
abscissa to the nearest integer, which is correct for standard GC-MS but
incorrect for high-resolution instruments (Orbitrap, Q-TOF) reporting accurate
masses to multiple decimal places.

\textit{Single-spectrum-per-page assumption.} Axis detection selects the longest
near-horizontal and near-vertical strokes on the page. Multi-panel figures
containing stacked spectra would cause the detector to span multiple plots.

\textit{Fidelity $\neq$ generalisation at scale.} The 99.99\,\% NIST result
measures round-trip fidelity under controlled rendering. The in-house experiment
(68 unique files, mean cosine 0.9963) provides cross-pipeline evidence on
independently produced spectra but, lacking an external reference and at a sample
size modest relative to the diversity of GC-MS instruments and reporting
conventions in the published literature, is not a measurement of accuracy.


% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion}

We have presented a two-tier pipeline for extracting $m/z$--intensity peak lists
from bar mass spectra in PDF documents. By reading the PDF vector drawing layer
directly, the common case requires no rasterization, no threshold tuning, and no
learned components, yielding near-lossless round-trip fidelity across 48,576 NIST
spectra (99.99\,\% at cosine $\geq 0.99$) and a mean vector--raster agreement
cosine of 0.9963 on 68 independently produced real-instrument spectra (68/68
base-peak agreement). A process-pool parallel driver achieves $6.94\times$
speedup (917\,PDFs/s, Amdahl ceiling $9.7\times$) by exploiting the embarrassing
parallelism of per-file independent extraction. The two observed NIST failures
--- both densely packed large-molecule spectra --- are identifiable by the
built-in validation layer and represent a well-defined failure mode amenable to
targeted improvement.

Future directions include: an OCR-based tick-label extraction path to handle
scanned documents outside both current tiers; sub-unit $m/z$ resolution to
support high-accuracy instruments; multi-panel figure detection for comparison
figures in the literature; and evaluation on a larger, more diverse sample of
third-party PDFs to characterise generalisation at publication scale. Beyond
extraction itself, the large machine-readable spectral datasets that this pipeline
enables are a natural input to the machine-learning models for compound
identification and spectrum prediction that currently face a training-data
bottleneck.


